1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,040
Imagine locking a camera above the Panama Canal

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,120
and refusing to move it for more than

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:05,660
a century.

4
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:08,380
At first, you would not see a canal

5
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:08,900
at all.

6
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,360
You would see jungle, river valleys, ridges, railroad

7
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:16,780
cuts, and a narrow isthmus separating two oceans.

8
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:21,260
Then, in the 1880s, excavation scars would begin

9
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:21,840
to appear.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,660
By 1914, the same landscape would have become

11
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:28,820
a water-powered machine of locks, dams, an

12
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:31,060
artificial lake, and a deep cut through the

13
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:32,120
continental divide.

14
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:35,200
A century later, the camera would catch another

15
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,840
transformation, a second generation of much larger locks

16
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:42,220
built beside the first system, officially opened in

17
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:43,080
2016.

18
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:46,460
That is the real story of the Panama

19
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:47,120
Canal.

20
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,120
Not a straight trench between two seas.

21
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,740
Not one construction project frozen in time.

22
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:57,440
It is a sequence of engineering decisions layered

23
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,960
onto the same geography, and each layer solved

24
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:01,940
a different problem.

25
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:05,140
The first builders tried to force a sea

26
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:06,640
-level passage through Panama.

27
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,620
The builders who followed changed the question entirely.

28
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,460
Instead of asking how to remove enough land

29
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:16,020
to keep ships at sea level, they asked

30
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,460
how water itself could lift ships over the

31
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:18,880
land.

32
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:22,840
Then, after the original canal had worked for

33
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:26,260
generations, Panama faced a new constraint.

34
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,680
Ships were becoming too large for the geometry

35
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,140
designed in the early twentieth century.

36
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,140
So keep that imaginary camera fixed, because if

37
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,340
we watch the same ground change from 1881

38
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:43,080
to 2016, the canal stops looking like a

39
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,180
single monument and starts looking like something more

40
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:46,760
interesting.

41
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:51,100
A machine that was redesigned whenever geography, technology,

42
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,720
and global shipping changed the rules.

43
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:58,140
In 1881, a French company led by Ferdinand

44
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,780
de Lesseps began work on a sea-level

45
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,160
canal across Panama.

46
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:05,860
The idea was easy to understand, because de

47
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,479
Lesseps had become famous for the Suez Canal.

48
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:11,440
If a channel could connect the Mediterranean and

49
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:14,240
Red Seas, why not cut another one across

50
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:15,160
Central America?

51
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,560
But on our fixed camera the difference would

52
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:19,440
become obvious.

53
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,440
Panama was not a flat desert corridor.

54
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:25,900
The route crossed a wet tropical landscape shaped

55
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:30,360
by heavy rainfall, rivers, unstable slopes, and a

56
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:33,460
continental divide that stood directly in the path

57
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:34,660
of the proposed channel.

58
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:37,760
A sea-level canal meant removing an extraordinary

59
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,660
amount of material, while also controlling water that

60
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:44,060
constantly wanted to enter the excavation.

61
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:48,840
The French effort left real work behind—cuts, machinery,

62
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,340
surveys, settlements, and a partially transformed corridor.

63
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:57,060
But the company collapsed in 1889, and work

64
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:57,540
stopped.

65
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:00,640
That failure is often treated like a preface

66
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,860
to the real canal.

67
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:04,720
Visually, it is more important than that.

68
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,180
It shows the first engineering philosophy written into

69
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:08,920
the landscape.

70
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,520
Make Panama conform to the canal.

71
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:13,300
Excavate enough.

72
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:14,520
Drain enough.

73
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:15,920
Cut low enough.

74
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:18,580
Keep the ships at sea level, and force

75
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:19,920
the land out of their way.

76
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:24,280
When the next construction era began, engineers inherited

77
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,120
both the physical remnants of that attempt and

78
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,760
the lesson hidden inside them.

79
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:32,120
The question was no longer simply, how do

80
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:33,260
we finish this trench?

81
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:36,520
The more powerful question was, what if the

82
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,820
trench is the wrong machine?

83
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,180
When United States construction began in 1904, the

84
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:46,020
project eventually committed to a fundamentally different solution—a

85
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:46,960
lake-and-lock canal.

86
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:50,960
That one decision changes almost everything our fixed

87
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:51,960
camera sees.

88
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:55,080
Instead of lowering the entire crossing to sea

89
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,720
level, the canal would raise ships from the

90
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,360
ocean to the level of Gatun Lake, carry

91
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:03,140
them across the interior, and lower them back

92
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:04,080
to the other ocean.

93
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:07,380
The original system does this in stages at

94
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:10,640
Gatun, Pedro Miguel, and Miraflores.

95
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:14,160
A ship enters a chamber, gates close behind

96
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:14,420
it.

97
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,380
Water flows by gravity.

98
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,440
The ship rises with the water, moves into

99
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,940
the next chamber, and repeats the process.

100
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:25,060
On the other side, the sequence reverses.

101
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:27,820
The ship is not climbing under its own

102
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:28,260
power.

103
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:31,200
The canal is changing the water level around

104
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:31,460
it.

105
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:34,540
At the high point, ships are roughly eighty

106
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:38,040
-five feet, or about twenty-six meters, above

107
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:38,720
sea level.

108
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:42,360
That means Gatun Lake is not just scenery

109
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:43,320
beside the canal.

110
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:45,360
It is part of the machine.

111
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,800
The original chambers were built in paired lanes

112
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,900
and measure one hundred ten feet wide by

113
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:53,320
one thousand feet long.

114
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:56,980
Those dimensions would later become famous because they

115
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:59,200
did more than define concrete walls.

116
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:03,040
They defined the maximum envelope around which generations

117
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,860
of commercial ships were designed.

118
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:07,040
Now return to the camera.

119
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:09,920
The landscape is no longer being treated as

120
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,640
an obstacle that must disappear.

121
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,000
Valleys become reservoirs.

122
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:15,820
Elevation becomes useful.

123
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:17,940
Gravity becomes infrastructure.

124
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:21,680
This is the first major reframe in the

125
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:23,200
canal's physical evolution.

126
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,340
The successful design did not defeat Panama's topography

127
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:28,800
by removing all of it.

128
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,720
It recruited the topography into the system.

129
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,620
But a clever diagram is not a working

130
00:05:34,620 --> 00:05:35,180
canal.

131
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,760
Between the idea and the first commercial transit

132
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:40,860
stood a much harder problem.

133
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,160
Building the machine in a landscape that was

134
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:49,000
still moving, flooding, eroding, and making people sick.

135
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,760
The next years turned the map into a

136
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:52,240
machine.

137
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:57,060
At Gatun, concrete starts defining enormous lock chambers.

138
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:01,140
On the Pacific side, Pedro Miguel and Miraflores

139
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:01,940
take shape.

140
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:06,680
Across the interior, excavation continues through what became

141
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:07,820
the Culebra Cut.

142
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:11,040
The engineering is easy to describe as separate

143
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:11,780
projects.

144
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:15,860
Build a dam, pour locks, excavate a cut,

145
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:18,460
control rivers, move spoil.

146
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:21,500
But they only make sense as one connected

147
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:22,000
system.

148
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,880
The lake has to reach the right level.

149
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:27,920
The cut has to connect into that lake.

150
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:30,660
The locks have to raise and lower ships

151
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:31,240
reliably.

152
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:34,060
Channels on both sides have to meet the

153
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:35,760
chambers at navigable depth.

154
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:38,320
And none of that matters if the workforce

155
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,500
cannot stay healthy enough to build it.

156
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:44,400
During the canal construction era, public health work

157
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,660
made major advances in controlling malaria and yellow

158
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:48,060
fever.

159
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:50,300
That was not a side story to the

160
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:50,780
engineering.

161
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:54,000
A project of this scale depended on people

162
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,180
being able to live and work in the

163
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:57,200
construction zone.

164
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:02,140
By 1913, the visual transformation becomes dramatic.

165
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:05,480
The cut has reached its construction era depth.

166
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:06,980
Water is rising.

167
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:10,420
Areas that once looked like valleys begin functioning

168
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:11,840
as a navigation lake.

169
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:15,040
The route that had been an excavation corridor

170
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:18,520
starts behaving like a continuous hydraulic system.

171
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:20,420
Then the pieces connect.

172
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:23,660
A ship entering from the Atlantic can be

173
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:26,940
lifted through Gatun, cross the lake, pass through

174
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:29,780
the cut, and descend toward the Pacific through

175
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:31,740
Pedro Miguel and Miraflores.

176
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:37,520
On August 15, 1914, the Panama Canal officially

177
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:38,640
opened to shipping.

178
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,540
Freeze the image there.

179
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:42,880
The canal now looks complete.

180
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,700
And for many histories, this is where the

181
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,140
engineering story peaks.

182
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:50,340
But that misses the most consequential idea.

183
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,480
The greatest innovation is not the cut through

184
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:54,100
the continent.

185
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:56,860
It is the decision to stop thinking of

186
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:58,760
the canal as a hole in the ground.

187
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:01,320
Here is the midpoint reframe.

188
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:04,440
The Panama Canal is not primarily a trench

189
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:06,380
that lets ships pass through land.

190
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:10,080
It is a controlled landscape that moves ships

191
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:11,640
through changes in elevation.

192
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:15,980
That difference explains why the successful canal could

193
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:19,580
cross Panama without excavating the entire route down

194
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:20,520
to ocean level.

195
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,020
The water is doing the heavy work.

196
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:26,540
Gravity moves water into and out of the

197
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:27,900
original lock chambers.

198
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:31,280
Gatun Lake stores the elevation that makes the

199
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:32,159
crossing possible.

200
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:35,340
The cut provides the passage through the divide,

201
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:37,840
but the lake and lock system determines how

202
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:39,820
a ship experiences that passage.

203
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:42,740
This is why a fixed camera view is

204
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:43,420
so revealing.

205
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:46,500
A normal travel shot follows a ship.

206
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:49,140
Our imaginary camera does the opposite.

207
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,420
It stays with the infrastructure.

208
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,980
The ship enters, rises, disappears across the lake,

209
00:08:56,420 --> 00:08:59,080
returns on the other side, and drops back

210
00:08:59,080 --> 00:08:59,780
to sea level.

211
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:02,780
Another ship follows, then another.

212
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:06,160
The moving object changes constantly.

213
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:08,040
The system remains.

214
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:11,180
And that system creates a second kind of

215
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:11,580
history.

216
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:15,620
Once the locks exist, global shipping begins adapting

217
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:16,220
to them.

218
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:19,880
The dimensions of the chambers become commercial constraints.

219
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:25,640
Routes, vessel sizes, schedules, and cargo strategies all

220
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:28,360
have to respect the physical geometry poured into

221
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,800
concrete before the First World War.

222
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:35,940
For decades, the canal's visible mechanism remains recognizable.

223
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:41,900
But ownership, administration, and the global shipping economy

224
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:44,320
around it do not stand still.

225
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:49,140
So the next transformation is strange because, at

226
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:52,140
first, it barely changes the camera view at

227
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:52,480
all.

228
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:54,560
The machine looks similar.

229
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:57,720
The authority over the machine changes.

230
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,680
The canal's next major transition is political rather

231
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:02,440
than geometric.

232
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:05,700
The Torrijos-Carter Treaties were signed on September

233
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:07,300
7, 1977.

234
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:11,140
Under the transition they established, the former canal

235
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:14,520
zone ceased to exist on October 1, 1979.

236
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:20,120
Two decades later, on December 31, 1999, the

237
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,840
canal was transferred to Panama.

238
00:10:22,620 --> 00:10:25,140
On our fixed camera, those dates do not

239
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:27,460
produce the kind of instant before-and-after

240
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:29,740
image that a new lock wall does.

241
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:31,640
The water still rises.

242
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,640
The gates still open.

243
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:36,600
Ships still cross Gatun Lake.

244
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:40,300
But infrastructure is never only concrete and steel.

245
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:43,260
It is also the institution that operates it,

246
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:47,040
maintains it, invests in it, and decides what

247
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,340
happens when the old design meets a new

248
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:49,900
constraint.

249
00:10:50,660 --> 00:10:54,140
By the time Panama assumed full control, that

250
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:56,640
constraint was becoming impossible to ignore.

251
00:10:57,140 --> 00:10:59,540
The original locks were built around a chamber

252
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:01,940
geometry from the early twentieth century.

253
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:05,000
Shipping had spent decades learning how to fit

254
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:05,860
that envelope.

255
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:09,520
But global commerce kept pushing toward larger vessels.

256
00:11:10,340 --> 00:11:13,460
The canal had survived because its core mechanism

257
00:11:13,460 --> 00:11:14,260
was durable.

258
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:18,360
Now that durability created a new problem.

259
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:21,520
If you keep the old locks, you keep

260
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:22,340
the old limit.

261
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:25,300
So the next question was no longer whether

262
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:27,900
Panama could operate the machine it inherited.

263
00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:31,300
It was whether Panama could enlarge the machine

264
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:34,600
without stopping the one already carrying traffic between

265
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:35,320
two oceans.

266
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:38,500
The answer was not to demolish the original

267
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:38,840
canal.

268
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,620
It was to build another generation beside it.

269
00:11:43,320 --> 00:11:46,160
The expansion added new lock complexes on the

270
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:48,000
Atlantic and Pacific sides.

271
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:51,120
More than one hundred fifty million cubic meters

272
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:53,920
of material were excavated as part of the

273
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:54,440
program.

274
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:58,740
The expanded waterway officially opened on June 26,

275
00:11:59,260 --> 00:11:59,940
2016.

276
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:04,580
Now the fixed-camera concept becomes almost literal.

277
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:07,880
The original locks remain part of the system,

278
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:09,820
still carrying traffic.

279
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:14,620
Nearby, new chambers create a second physical vocabulary,

280
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:18,100
larger lock structures built for larger vessels.

281
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:20,600
The difference is not subtle.

282
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:25,060
According to the Panama Canal Authority, the expanded

283
00:12:25,060 --> 00:12:28,400
locks are seventy feet wider and eighteen feet

284
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:30,280
deeper than the original locks.

285
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:34,960
But larger chambers create another engineering problem, water.

286
00:12:35,700 --> 00:12:38,560
Every lockage moves a huge volume of water.

287
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:42,680
Simply scaling the old concept upward would increase

288
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:43,320
the demand.

289
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:47,300
The new locks therefore use water-saving basins

290
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:50,340
that reuse about sixty percent of the water

291
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:51,520
used per transit.

292
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:55,500
That detail reveals how much the design philosophy

293
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:56,320
has evolved.

294
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:00,060
In the first successful canal, the breakthrough was

295
00:13:00,060 --> 00:13:02,980
learning to use water and gravity instead of

296
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:05,660
trying to excavate the entire crossing to sea

297
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:05,960
level.

298
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,340
In the expanded canal, the challenge was learning

299
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:12,080
to move larger ships while managing that water

300
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:12,820
more carefully.

301
00:13:13,530 --> 00:13:14,480
Same geography.

302
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:18,000
Same basic idea of lifting ships across a

303
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:19,300
higher interior route.

304
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:21,720
Different generation of infrastructure.

305
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:25,300
And this is why calling the 2016 project

306
00:13:25,300 --> 00:13:29,200
an expansion can undersell what the camera actually

307
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:29,820
sees.

308
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:33,100
It is a new machine operating beside an

309
00:13:33,100 --> 00:13:36,520
old one, connected into the same continental crossing.

310
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:39,640
The original system did not become obsolete.

311
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:43,180
It became one layer in a larger system.

312
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:46,080
Return to the camera one last time.

313
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:49,260
The jungle corridor of the nineteenth century is

314
00:13:49,260 --> 00:13:51,320
still underneath everything we have watched.

315
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:54,120
So are traces of the French ambition to

316
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:55,300
cut a sea-level route.

317
00:13:56,060 --> 00:13:58,280
Then comes the decisive redesign.

318
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:03,400
Locks, Gatun Lake, controlled elevation, and gravity turning

319
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:05,920
water into the force that lifts ships across

320
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:06,600
the isthmus.

321
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:09,320
Then, a century of operation.

322
00:14:10,020 --> 00:14:11,400
A transfer of control.

323
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:14,720
And finally, a new set of locks beside

324
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:17,780
the old ones, larger in scale and designed

325
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:20,640
to reuse water in ways the original builders

326
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:21,800
never had to imagine.

327
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:26,240
From that fixed viewpoint, the Panama Canal stops

328
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:29,360
looking like a single construction project, with a

329
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:30,540
beginning and an end.

330
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:33,920
It looks like an argument between generations of

331
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:36,340
engineers and the same piece of land.

332
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:39,740
The French approach said, lower the land until

333
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:40,940
the ships can pass.

334
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:44,040
The Lock and Lake approach said, raise the

335
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:44,900
ships instead.

336
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,380
The expansion said, keep the proven mechanism, but

337
00:14:49,380 --> 00:14:51,820
build a larger parallel system for a new

338
00:14:51,820 --> 00:14:52,640
shipping world.

339
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:55,060
That pattern is bigger than Panama.

340
00:14:55,980 --> 00:14:58,840
Infrastructure lasts when it can survive one era

341
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:01,320
long enough to be reinterpreted by the next.

342
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:05,020
The most impressive thing about the canal is

343
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:07,400
not that people once cut a route between

344
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:08,120
two oceans.

345
00:15:08,860 --> 00:15:11,460
It is that the route kept changing without

346
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:14,700
abandoning the geographic problem that created it.

347
00:15:15,220 --> 00:15:16,460
One strip of land.

348
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:19,020
Three engineering logics.

349
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:22,180
And more than a century of redesign written

350
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:23,520
into the same landscape.

351
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:26,680
That is what the fixed camera would really

352
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:27,180
capture.

353
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:30,440
Not a canal being built once, but a

354
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:32,540
system learning how to remain useful.
